      "message": "  AVX2 uses 256-bit (32-byte) YMM registers.\n  \n  • Input (ARGB4444): 2 bytes per pixel. To fill one 32-byte YMM register for the initial load ( vmovdqu ), we need exactly  32 bytes / 2 bytes/pixel \u003d  \n  16 pixels .\n  • Output (ARGB): 4 bytes per pixel. 16 pixels require  16 * 4 \u003d 64 bytes  of output. This is why the assembly/intrinsic version performs two 32-byte   \n  writes (stores) per iteration:\n    vmovdqu     %%ymm2,(%1,%0,2)              # Store first 32 bytes (8 pixels)\n    vmovdqu     %%ymm1,0x20(%1,%0,2)          # Store next 32 bytes (8 pixels)\n  \n\n  ### 3. Tail Handling (Any Version)\n  \n  In  source/row_any.cc , the fallback handler is defined as:\n  \n    ANY11(ARGB4444ToARGBRow_Any_AVX2, ARGB4444ToARGBRow_AVX2, 0, 2, 4, 15)\n  \n  The last parameter  15  is the mask ( alignment - 1 ), which also confirms that the SIMD function expects blocks of  15 + 1 \u003d 16  pixels.              \n  \n  ### Conclusion\n  \n  Since the optimized AVX2 loop strictly processes data in steps of 16 pixels, the  width  must be a multiple of 16 to safely use the fully optimized    \n  ARGB4444ToARGBRow_AVX2  without overflowing the buffers. If the width is not a multiple of 16, the  ARGB4444ToARGBRow_Any_AVX2  wrapper is correctly   \n  selected to handle the remaining pixels.",
